### 1. Levenshtein Distance
Levenshtein distance measures the minimum number of single-character edits (insertions, deletions, or substitutions) required to change one string into another. This is particularly useful for comparing strings where the differences are small but the strings are not exactly identical.

#### Example in PHP:
```php
function levenshtein($a, $b) {
    $len_a = strlen($a);
    $len_b = strlen($b);
    $dp = [];

    for ($i = 0; $i <= $len_a; $i++) {
        $dp[$i] = [$i];
    }
    for ($j = 0; $j <= $len_b; $j++) {
        $dp[0][$j] = $j;
    }

    for ($i = 1; $i <= $len_a; $i++) {
        for ($j = 1; $j <= $len_b; $j++) {
            if ($a[$i - 1] == $b[$j - 1]) {
                $dp[$i][$j] = $dp[$i - 1][$j - 1];
            } else {
                $dp[$i][$j] = min(
                    $dp[$i - 1][$j] + 1,      // deletion
                    $dp[$i][$j - 1] + 1,      // insertion
                    $dp[$i - 1][$j - 1] + 1   // substitution
                );
            }
        }
    }

    return $dp[$len_a][$len_b];
}

