@Path("/user")
public class UserService {

    @POST
    @Path("/register")
    @Consumes(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
    @Produces(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
    public Response registerUser(@Valid UserRegistrationBean userRegistrationBean) {
        // Process the userRegistrationBean
        // Handle validation errors if any
        return Response.ok(userRegistrationBean).build();
    }
}
```

In this example, `UserRegistrationBean` must have a `@Valid` annotation to be validated when it is submitted via a POST request.

### Validator and Error Response:
- If a validation error occurs, Spring MVC will return an error response with the validation errors contained in the response body, indicating which fields are invalid.

### Important Considerations:
- The parameter being validated (`userRegistrationBean` in the example) should include field-level annotations that define the validation rules.
- If you have multiple levels of validation, you may need to use `<mvc:annotation-driven>` in your Spring configuration to enable them.

By using `@Valid`, you can control the data entering your application more effectively, ensuring that your logic is only applied to valid data.
